  "method": "For seven GBIF backbone classes and each of seven GBIF continents, one faceted occurrence search (coordinates present, no geospatial issue, fossil and living specimens excluded) returned record counts per genus, family (and species for the smaller classes). Europe and Asia were merged into Eurasia. Taxa with >= 20 records were scored as confined if >= 95% of records fell in one region, and the fraction of confined taxa was compared between genera and families within each class. For mammals, genus crown ages were computed as the MRCA age of sampled congeners in the Upham et al. 2019 DNA-only MCC tree, genus names were matched to GBIF genus keys, and crown age was correlated (Spearman, and partial Spearman controlling log sampled richness) with the number of regions holding >= 5% of the genus's records; single-region fractions were compared between youngest and oldest crown-age terciles.",
